How Healenium Actually Works Internally
Let me break this down properly because the internals are important for you to understand what you're integrating — and to explain it to your manager.
The Architecture (3 pieces)
Healenium has a client part and a backend part. The backend is a server that uses a PostgreSQL database, which performs interactions between the client part, the tree-comparing algorithm, and the database that stores the old and new locator values along with related information like DOM page, method name, class name, screenshots, etc.
Automate The Planet
So there are three components running:
1. Your test code — your existing Selenium+Java+Gradle project. The only change is wrapping ChromeDriver with SelfHealingDriver. All your page objects, test classes, assertions stay exactly the same.
2. Healenium Backend — a Spring Boot service running in Docker. This is the brain. It stores locator history, runs the ML comparison algorithm, and serves a healing report UI.
3. PostgreSQL Database — also in Docker. Stores every locator it has ever seen, the DOM snapshots, and the healing history.
What Happens at Runtime (step by step)
Healenium sits as a Self-Healing Driver (Proxy) in front of the real driver. Every findElement, click, sendKeys call is intercepted. Locator details (ID, CSS, XPath, tag, class, text, etc.) are stored. 
Medium
Here's the exact flow:
First run (learning mode): Your test runs normally. Every time driver.findElement(By.id("loginBtn")) is called and succeeds, Healenium captures a snapshot — the locator used, the DOM tree around that element, its attributes, text, position, parent-child relationships — and stores it all in PostgreSQL. Nothing changes in behavior. Your test passes as usual. This is Healenium building its knowledge base.
Subsequent run (healing mode): Say a developer changed id="loginBtn" to id="login-button-new". Your test calls findElement(By.id("loginBtn")). The real Selenium throws NoSuchElementException. But instead of your test crashing, Healenium catches the NoSuchElement exception, triggers the Machine Learning algorithm, passes the current page state, gets the previous successful locator path, compares them, and generates a list of healed locators. It takes the locator with the highest score and performs an action with this locator. 
Medium
The algorithm works by comparing the DOM tree structure from the last successful run to the current DOM tree. It uses a tree-comparison algorithm (similar to how Git diffs work, but for HTML trees) to find which element in the new DOM most closely matches the element it previously found. It scores candidates based on tag name, attributes, text content, position in the tree, sibling elements, and parent hierarchy. The score-cap setting (default 0.5) means healing will be performed only for new healed locators where probability of match with the target one is >= 50%. 
GitHub
Does it change your code? No. Healenium does NOT modify your test scripts or Page Object Model files. It does not change the XPath in your BaseTest.java or LoginPage.java or any of your source files. Instead, it updates locators dynamically at runtime. It's purely a runtime proxy — your source code stays untouched. 
Medium
But you CAN update your code afterwards. The IntelliJ IDEA plugin searches for healing results in the DB and updates the test code with the healed locator. So after a run where healing happened, you open IntelliJ, right-click on the healed locator, and it shows you what the new locator is — you accept it and your code is permanently fixed too. 
